            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new word: what did dhanyaḥ mean, and what was inside dhanyavādaḥ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: द्वारम्",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "द्वारम् (dvāram) — \"a door, a gate\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A guest is met at the द्वारम् (dvāram) — the door, the gate."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is one of the securest cousins in the book. Latin foris and forēs, \"door\" — whence forum, the space outside the door, and foreign, what is out of doors. Greek thura. English door, German Tür, Russian dver. The word for the opening of a house survived every migration intact, which is what you would expect of a word every generation says every day. Indian place names keep it too: Haridwar is Hari's gate."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A door, and a word that reached every branch of the family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
